sentences.l:
He has raced the glance with a thoroughbred, a swift one, * as if it wanted to overtake the Decree;
its blackness shows a blackness pitch-dark, * as if it were night when the night grows thick;
its neighing delights whoever hears it, * as if it were the thunder when the thunder rolls;
if it raced the wind it would run ahead of it, * and the lightning does not outstrip it when it appears.

sentences.i:
He has raced the very glance of the eye on a swift thoroughbred * that seems to want to overtake fate itself;
its coat shows a blackness deep as pitch, * like night when the night is thickest;
its neighing thrills all who hear it, * like thunder when the thunder rolls;
if it raced the wind it would be there first, * and lightning, when it flashes, could not outrun it.
